def _s(title, text, actions=(), help=None):
    return {"title": title, "text": text, "actions": list(actions), "help": help}


TOUR_DRZAK = [
    _s("Example 1 – printed bracket",
       "<p>A single PLA part printed on a Bambu: a vertical plate screwed to a wall with two screws, a horizontal arm with an Ø8 eye and a rib. A 200 N load hangs on the eye.</p><p>The project opens with its results (a copy in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\FEAStudio\\ukazky</code>, the original stays untouched). The <b>Show in app</b> button always puts the program into the state the step talks about; <b>Next</b> moves on.</p><p class='tip'><b>Tip:</b> 3D window controls: middle button = rotate, right = pan, wheel = zoom, <span class='key'>F</span> = whole model. Details in <a href='ovladani.html'>Controls</a>.</p>",
       [("open", U1), ("stage", "Model")], "ukazky"),
    _s("Material – printed PLA",
       "<p>The part has the material <b>Tisk PLA Bambu Basic</b>. A printed material has two sets of properties: in the layer plane (E, Rm – XY axes) and perpendicular to the layers (E_z, Rm_z – Z axis). The values apply to <i>solid</i> extrusion; the program computes the perimeters and infill from them itself.</p><p>A part with a printed material is always computed as 3D printing.</p>",
       [("stage", "Materials")], "materialy"),
    _s("3D printing – part settings",
       "<p>The <b>3D printing</b> step: build direction Z (the part lies on the bed as modeled), 3 perimeters × 0.42 mm, 5/3 top/bottom layers × 0.2 mm, gyroid infill 20 %.</p><p>The model is <b>homogenized</b>: elements near the surface are shell (perimeters, solid layers), inside is a core with the effective infill properties. Elements at the interface get a mixture according to the shell fraction.</p><p class='warn'><b>Warning:</b> Layers perpendicular to the main tension are weak (Z typically 50–70 % of XY strength). Here the tension in the upper part of the arm runs along the layers – good. Try changing the build direction to Y (printing on its side) and compare the safety factor.</p>",
       [("stage", "3D printing"), ("list", "print_panel.parts", "Drzak")], "tisk"),
    _s("Shell and core in section",
       "<p>The <b>Shell / core</b> button shows a mesh section colored by the shell fraction in the element (1 = solid shell, 0 = infill only). Use the slider to move the section plane.</p><p class='tip'><b>Tip:</b> The shell is ~1.3 mm thick and the mesh elements are 2.5 mm – the shell stress therefore depends on the mesh. Refine the mesh at the critical location (Mesh step → local refinement).</p>",
       [("stage", "3D printing"), ("need_mesh",), ("call", "print_panel.show_shell")], "tisk"),
    _s("Supports – screws and bearing against the wall",
       "<p>Each screw hole has a <b>cylindrical support</b> (zero radial, circumferential and axial displacement) – a cylindrical support takes one axis, hence two. The rear face has <b>ux = 0</b> (bearing against the wall; the program has no contacts, so the bearing holds in tension too).</p>",
       [("stage", "Constraints")], "vazby"),
    _s("Loads – bearing load on the eye",
       "<p>Case <b>Eye load 200 N</b>: <i>bearing load</i> = pressure with a cosine distribution on the half of the eye facing the force (like a pin in a hole). The second case is self-weight.</p><p>Each load case is a separate analysis; fatigue combines them by superposition.</p>",
       [("stage", "Loads")], "zatizeni"),
    _s("Mesh",
       "<p>Tet10 elements (quadratic tetrahedra), size 2.5 mm. A min. scaled Jacobian above 0.1 is fine.</p>",
       [("stage", "Mesh")], "sit"),
    _s("Solve",
       "<p>The <b>Solve</b> step checks readiness (materials, supports, rigid-body motion) and runs CalculiX. After the analysis, the equilibrium of forces and moments is always checked – an imbalance above 0.1 % = error.</p><p>For a printed part with creep, a second analysis is run on the same mesh with long-term (isochronous) properties.</p><p><b>Show in app</b> runs the analysis (under a minute) if the example does not have results yet. After it finishes, <span class='key'>Ctrl+S</span> saves the example with the results – next time the guide offers to continue with them.</p>",
       [("stage", "Solve"), ("solve",)], "reseni"),
    _s("Results – von Mises",
       "<p>The eye displacement is about 0.6 mm, von Mises at most ~18 MPa at the sharp inner corner where the rib meets the wall (a corner without a fillet = singularity, the value grows with mesh refinement). For a printed part, however, the <b>print safety factor</b> (next step) is what governs – von Mises knows nothing about the weak Z direction or weak infill.</p><p>Clicking into the model shows the value (probe). The <b>Highest stresses</b> table classifies the maxima locations (singularity × real location).</p>",
       [("stage", "Results"), ("need_results",), ("combo", "r_step", "Zatížení oka|Eye load"), ("combo", "r_qty", "Von Mises")], "vysledky"),
    _s("Results – 3D printing safety factor",
       "<p><b>3D printing – safety factor</b> = load multiplier at which the Tsai-Wu criterion reaches 1 (failure), computed separately in the shell and in the core, the node takes the worse one.</p><p>Here it comes out at about <b>1.9</b> and it is governed by the <b>infill (core) inside the wall</b> at the arm level – not the surface, where the stress is highest. On the surface the map shows higher values; find the minimum inside with a section (top bar: Section → Y, slider) or in the summary below the legend. Gyroid 20 % has only ~7 % of the strength of solid extrusion. Try 40 % infill or 4 perimeters in the 3D printing step and recompute.</p><p class='warn'><b>Warning:</b> For PLA design for a safety factor of at least ~2 (print scatter, creep, moisture).</p>",
       [("stage", "Results"), ("need_results",), ("combo", "r_step", "Zatížení oka|Eye load"), ("combo", "r_qty", "3D printing – safety")],
       "tisk"),
    _s("Creep – 1000 h at 30 °C",
       "<p>Case <b>“… – creep 1000 h, 30 °C”</b> = the same load with the long-term modulus (Findley) and reduced strength (creep rupture). Displacement grows about 2× (0.6 → 1.2 mm), the safety factor drops to ~1.3 – below the required 1.5; after 1000 h of the hanging load the bracket would not pass.</p><p class='warn'><b>Warning:</b> Creep is only a linear estimate (indicative data). The report always warns about it in red.</p>",
       [("stage", "Results"), ("need_results",), ("combo", "r_step", "creep|tečení"), ("combo", "r_qty", "Total displacement")], "tisk"),
    _s("How to strengthen the bracket – advice and sweep",
       "<p>The <b>3D printing</b> step, section <b>► Advice by failure location and build direction</b> (click to expand) → <b>Advise</b>: the program proposes a change based on the weakest location – here the infill governs, so it advises a higher density. <b>Sweep infill</b> prefills the <b>► Parameter sweep</b> section.</p><p>The sweep computes combinations of perimeters and infill on a single mesh and shows a mass × safety factor chart with the Pareto front; it recommends the lightest variant with a safety factor ≥ 1.5. <b>Rank build directions</b> computes the part in all 6 axes. Everything is indicative – verify the benefit with a variant.</p>",
       [("stage", "3D printing")], "tisk"),
    _s("Fatigue – pulsating load",
       "<p>The <b>Fatigue</b> step: the “sinus” channel multiplies the Eye load case by values 0–1 (pulsating 0–200 N), 100 000 repetitions. The Compute button produces a damage / life / safety factor map; printed parts use the Hill equivalent in the print axes and the print S-N curve.</p>",
       [("stage", "Fatigue")], "unava"),
    _s("Protokol do Wordu",
       "<p><span class='key'>Ctrl+P</span> creates a technical report .docx: setup, mesh, results with maps, 3D printing (settings, safety factor, creep), fatigue, assumptions and conclusion.</p><p>Continue with <a href='fea:pruvodce2'>Example 2 – assembly</a> with bolts, welds and heat.</p>",
       [], "protokol"),
]

